// MAX HIT SPLATS (2026-09-27), Old School's: a hit that rolls the attacker's max hit is drawn with the
// red splat with the gold rim (hitmarks 7, ^hitmark_max) instead of the plain red one. Old School
// added it long after 2006 and has a setting to turn it off; here it is always on.
//
// "The max hit" is the number the damage was rolled against, after everything that changes it - the
// slayer mask, Dharok's, the monster's max_dealt cap, the 40% a protection prayer takes off in PvP - so
// a hit on a player praying against it is a max hit when it is the most the prayer let through. A hit
// is flagged when it lands at least that high, which also covers a bolt effect that rolls past it.
//
// Covered: a player's melee, ranged, magic and toxic blowpipe shots on a monster; a monster's default
// melee, ranged and magic attacks on a player; and a player's melee, ranged, magic and blowpipe shots
// on another player. Special attacks, bosses' own attack scripts and the other odd damage sources keep
// the plain splat - they go through the old calls, which never pass a max.
//
// HOW IT RIDES THE QUEUE. The splat is decided where the damage is rolled and drawn where it lands,
// ticks later, through a queue:
//   * on a monster, by the engine: npc_queue_maxhit is npc_queue, marked, and the one npc_damage of the
//     damage type for exactly the queued amount in the ai_queue it runs becomes the max hit splat. That
//     is what lets every [ai_queue2,...] - the default and the monsters' own - draw it without a change;
//     one that alters the damage first just draws the plain splat.
//   * on a player, by the queue itself: [queue,combat_maxhit_player] beside [queue,combat_damage_player],
//     and [queue,pvp_damage] carries the hitmark as a third argument.
// An old client, from before these splats, is sent the plain red one instead (the engine's
// HitType.legacyHitType), so it sees what it always did.

// A player's hit on the active monster, $delay ticks away - npc_queue(2, $damage, $delay) otherwise.
[proc,npc_queue_hit](int $damage, int $maxhit, int $delay)
if ($maxhit > 0 & $damage >= $maxhit) {
    npc_queue_maxhit(2, $damage, $delay);
} else {
    npc_queue(2, $damage, $delay);
}

// The active monster's hit on the active player - queue(combat_damage_player, $delay, $damage) otherwise.
[proc,queue_combat_damage_player](int $delay, int $damage, int $maxhit)
if ($maxhit > 0 & $damage >= $maxhit) {
    queue(combat_maxhit_player, $delay, $damage);
} else {
    queue(combat_damage_player, $delay, $damage);
}

[queue,combat_maxhit_player](int $damage)
~combat_damage_player_hit($damage, ^hitmark_max);
